ikes-to-views ratio; it should typically fall between 3% and 7% for healthy content. Look at the comments. If they are generic phrases like \"Great video!\" or \"Nice!\", they are likely automated. You want to see specific, relevant conversations that prove a real human connection exists. Recent upload performance is the ultimate truth. If the last three videos have failed to reach 5% of the subscriber count in views, the audience has likely moved on.\n\n### Checking Channel Health and History\n\nA clean record is non-negotiable. Check the \"Copyright\" and \"Community Guidelines\" sections in the YouTube Studio. Any active strikes can lead to immediate termination if a second mistake occurs. You should also investigate the niche history. If a channel has pivoted from gaming to beauty to tech, the algorithm is confused and the audience is fractured. Consistency builds value. Finally, ensure the \"Monetization\" tab is active and green. If it\'s disabled for \"reused content\" or \"invalid click activity,\" the channel is a liability. You aren\'t just buying a name; you\'re buying a clean reputation.\n\n## <a name=\"the-risks-of-direct-transfers-vs-secure-escrow\"></a>The Risks of Direct Transfers vs. Secure Escrow\n\nDirect transactions are a security failure waiting to happen. When you attempt to **buy youtube channel for monetization** through a private deal, you have zero leverage. The most common scam is the \"payment first\" trap. A seller requests a direct bank transfer or UPI payment, then disappears. Even worse is account reclamation. A seller hands over the credentials, waits for the payment to clear, and then uses Google\'s account recovery tools to \"prove\" they were hacked. They get the money and the channel back. You are left with nothing but a transaction ID and a blocked contact.\n\nTraditional payment methods like PayPal or standard bank transfers offer a false sense of security. PayPal\'s buyer protection frequently excludes intangible digital asse