r rather than a legitimate transition. Payment reversals plague peer-to-peer deals. Scammers often use PayPal chargebacks or stolen credit cards to fund a purchase, only to claw the money back once they have the credentials. You should also watch for credential phishing. Fake escrow sites are designed to look like a professional **secure account handover service** but exist only to steal your login data and your funds.\\n\\n### Understanding Platform Terms of Service (ToS)\\n\\nPlatforms like YouTube and Instagram don\'t officially support the sale of accounts. If you mention \\\"buying\\\" or \\\"selling\\\" in a support chat, you risk an instant ban. You must frame the transition as a change in management or a corporate reorganization. Documenting these steps is essential to meet [legal requirements for social media account transfers](https://navigantlaw.com/social-media-and-your-business-steps-to-take-to-document-the-transfer-of-a-social-media-account/). Proper documentation protects your investment if the platform ever questions the ownership change. It turns a \\\"gray market\\\" deal into a professional business move.\\n\\n### The Vulnerability of 2FA and Recovery Info\\n\\nMulti-factor authentication (2FA) is not a silver bullet. Removing a seller\'s phone number is a start, but it isn\'t a finish. Backdoor access often remains through linked third-party apps like Hootsuite, Canva, or older \\\"authorized\\\" devices. If the seller maintains these links, they maintain control. You must secure the original registration email (OGE) and clear all legacy sessions to be truly safe. This complexity is why many professionals choose a [verified marketplace with built-in protection](https://accsmarkets.org) to handle the heavy lifting. It ensures that every technical thread is cut before the deal is finalized.\\n\\n## <a name=\\\"step-by-step-how-to-execute-a-secure-handover-in-2026\\\"></a>Step-by-Step: How to Execute a Secure Handover in 2026\\n\\nExecuting a succes