infrastructure layer. It\'s the only way to ensure accountability. This infrastructure acts as a digital guardian, monitoring the handover and securing the transaction until the buyer has full control.</p>\n\n<h3>The Value Proposition of Established Pages</h3>\n\n<p>Starting a new page in 2026 is a slow process. Meta\'s algorithm now prioritizes original content and established history. New accounts often face a \"sandbox\" period where reach is restricted. Buying an existing page allows you to bypass this phase immediately. The benefits are clear:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate Reach:</strong> You gain instant access to a targeted demographic without the cost of initial follower acquisition ads.</li>\n<li><strong>Monetization Readiness:</strong> Many listings in a <b>facebook page marketplace</b> are already enrolled in the unified Content Monetization Program. This includes access to in-stream ads and Reels revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Algorithmic Trust:</strong> Older pages with a history of original posting are favored by current distribution models.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The market has shifted. Monetized pages are no longer just social profiles; they\'re high-yield digital assets. Professional buyers treat these acquisitions like real estate. They look for verified health metrics and clean ownership records. Relying on unmonitored peer-to-peer deals is a liability. You need a system that offers transparency in page health and protects your capital through a staged transaction workflow.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"critical-metrics-for-evaluating-marketplace-listings\">Critical Metrics for Evaluating Marketplace Listings</h2>\n\n<p>Don\'t be fooled by vanity metrics. A high follower count is easy to fake and often hides a dying asset. Professional buyers look for engagement depth and audience authenticity. In any <b>facebook page marketplace</b>, the listing description is just the starting point. You need raw data to verify the asset\'s health before committing capital. Buyin