ud and spam. According to [Facebook\'s terms of service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criticism_of_Facebook), transferring ownership without explicit permission is technically prohibited. This creates a massive \"grey market\" where transactions happen in the shadows. The platform uses sophisticated pattern recognition to spot these deals. A sudden change in admin location, a shift in IP address from a different region, or a total change in content category triggers an internal flag. These flags often lead to the \"Page Restricted\" notification that every buyer fears. You aren\'t just buying a page; you\'re inheriting its entire digital history, both good and bad.\n\n## <a name=\"the-primary-risks-of-buying-facebook-pages-from-bans-to-scams\"></a>The Primary Risks of Buying Facebook Pages: From Bans to Scams\n\nBuying a page isn\'t a simple hand-off. The [black market for Facebook accounts](https://www.techtransparencyproject.org/articles/facebook-black-market-ad-accounts-raises-new-scam-election-interference-fears) is riddled with pitfalls that go beyond surface-level scams. You aren\'t just buying followers. You\'re buying the page\'s history and the seller\'s honesty. In unmoderated environments like Telegram or WhatsApp groups, the risk of financial fraud is nearly 100%. Sellers demand \"payment first\" and vanish. Even if you get the page, the technical **risks of buying facebook pages** can still liquidate your investment within hours.\n\n### The \'Page Recovery\' Trap\n\nOwnership reversion is the process where a seller reclaims a page after receiving payment by exploiting Meta’s account recovery tools. This is a primary risk in 2026. A seller waits for the transaction to clear. They then file a \"hacked account\" report with Facebook support. They provide original ID verification or early account details that you, as a new buyer, cannot match. Facebook restores the page to the \"rightful\" owner. You lose the asset and your money. To mitigate this, you mus